1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:07,410
In March, nineteen nineteen, a letter told New Orleans how to live through the night.

2
00:00:07,459 --> 00:00:09,599
Play jazz.

3
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:11,229
The city did.

4
00:00:11,279 --> 00:00:13,969
No one was killed that night.

5
00:00:14,019 --> 00:00:17,669
It is the most famous thing about the Axeman.

6
00:00:17,719 --> 00:00:20,500
And it may be the one thing in the case

7
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:21,760
he never wrote.

8
00:00:26,519 --> 00:00:31,070
This is the case of the Axeman of New Orleans.

9
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:34,780
Nineteen eighteen and nineteen nineteen.

10
00:00:34,829 --> 00:00:36,979
Grocers, and their families, attacked in their homes at night.

11
00:00:37,030 --> 00:00:39,840
No conviction for these attacks ever stood.

12
00:00:39,890 --> 00:00:43,609
I'm not going to tell you who did it.

13
00:00:43,659 --> 00:00:48,420
I'm going to show you the record.

14
00:00:48,469 --> 00:00:52,850
The newspapers of the time, and the court cases that came after.

15
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:57,539
And I'm going to show you where that record splits in two.

16
00:00:57,590 --> 00:00:59,520
Welcome to story timez.

17
00:00:59,570 --> 00:01:02,190
If you like your true crime told from the record,

18
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:03,480
subscribe,

19
00:01:03,530 --> 00:01:06,379
and tell me in the comments which case I should open next.

20
00:01:07,006 --> 00:01:11,406
Tuesday, March eighteenth, nineteen nineteen.

21
00:01:11,456 --> 00:01:16,606
At twelve fifteen that night, the first minutes of March nineteenth,

22
00:01:16,656 --> 00:01:19,416
someone had promised to come.

23
00:01:19,466 --> 00:01:22,516
March nineteenth is Saint Joseph's Day.

24
00:01:22,566 --> 00:01:29,896
In New Orleans, many of the families who had been attacked were Sicilian and Italian,

25
00:01:29,946 --> 00:01:31,586
and Saint Joseph's Day was theirs.

26
00:01:31,636 --> 00:01:35,556
The promise had been printed two days earlier, on a Sunday.

27
00:01:35,606 --> 00:01:38,856
It said one thing would keep a house safe.

28
00:01:38,906 --> 00:01:41,656
A jazz band, playing.

29
00:01:41,706 --> 00:01:45,576
According to the city's newspaper, the dance halls filled.

30
00:01:45,626 --> 00:01:48,856
Bands played at house parties across town.

31
00:01:48,906 --> 00:01:51,216
Nobody was attacked.

32
00:01:51,266 --> 00:01:54,666
That night is the reason most people know this case.

33
00:01:54,716 --> 00:01:56,546
So before we read the letter,

34
00:01:56,596 --> 00:01:58,346
let's look at what came before it.

35
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:06,733
May, nineteen eighteen. The twenty-second or the twenty-third; accounts differ.

36
00:02:06,783 --> 00:02:12,163
Joseph Maggio ran a grocery at the corner of Upperline and Magnolia.

37
00:02:12,213 --> 00:02:15,733
He and his wife, Catherine, lived behind the store.

38
00:02:15,783 --> 00:02:20,473
Early that morning, his brothers, Jake and Andrew, found them.

39
00:02:20,523 --> 00:02:22,073
Joseph was dying. Catherine was dead.

40
00:02:22,123 --> 00:02:28,303
Their throats had been cut. They had been struck with an axe.

41
00:02:28,353 --> 00:02:30,943
Money was left where it could be seen.

42
00:02:30,993 --> 00:02:34,113
A straight razor was found on a neighbor's lawn.

43
00:02:34,163 --> 00:02:37,763
It belonged to Andrew Maggio, who was a barber.

44
00:02:37,813 --> 00:02:39,333
Andrew was arrested.

45
00:02:39,383 --> 00:02:40,743
He was released.

46
00:02:40,793 --> 00:02:43,833
A panel had been chiseled out of the back door.

47
00:02:43,883 --> 00:02:48,553
And on the pavement nearby, accounts describe a message in chalk.

48
00:02:48,603 --> 00:02:52,483
Mrs. Maggio will sit up tonight, just like Mrs. Toney.

49
00:02:52,533 --> 00:02:55,473
The wording changes a little from book to book.

50
00:02:55,523 --> 00:02:56,923
Remember that panel.

51
00:02:56,973 --> 00:02:58,303
It comes back.

52
00:02:58,353 --> 00:03:00,863
August tenth, nineteen eighteen.

53
00:03:00,913 --> 00:03:04,703
Joseph Romano, an elderly man, was attacked in his home.

54
00:03:04,753 --> 00:03:09,353
His nieces, Pauline and Mary Bruno, saw a man in the house.

55
00:03:09,403 --> 00:03:13,793
He was heavy-set, they said, in a dark suit and a slouch hat.

56
00:03:13,843 --> 00:03:16,833
A panel of the back door had been chiseled out.

57
00:03:16,883 --> 00:03:19,123
A bloody axe was in the yard.

58
00:03:19,173 --> 00:03:20,933
Nothing had been taken.

59
00:03:20,983 --> 00:03:23,543
Joseph Romano died of his head wounds.

60
00:03:23,593 --> 00:03:26,543
Some accounts say two days later. Some say three.

61
00:03:27,164 --> 00:03:30,594
By August, the papers were counting.

62
00:03:30,644 --> 00:03:34,334
And here is the first place the record splits.

63
00:03:34,384 --> 00:03:38,214
Between those two killings, there were other attacks.

64
00:03:38,264 --> 00:03:44,104
June twenty-seventh. A grocer named Louis Besumer, and a woman named Harriet Lowe,

65
00:03:44,154 --> 00:03:47,934
found hurt behind his store, with a hatchet.

66
00:03:47,984 --> 00:03:51,404
Besumer was later charged with murder himself.

67
00:03:51,454 --> 00:03:55,204
A jury acquitted him in May, nineteen nineteen.

68
00:03:55,254 --> 00:03:59,694
August fifth. A pregnant woman, the wife of Edward Schneider,

69
00:03:59,744 --> 00:04:03,744
found struck in her bed by her husband, after midnight.

70
00:04:03,794 --> 00:04:08,284
She lived. Two days later she gave birth to a daughter.

71
00:04:08,334 --> 00:04:12,674
The papers put these attacks on the same list as the Maggios.

72
00:04:12,724 --> 00:04:19,254
One modern historian, Miriam Davis, who went back to coroner's records and the papers,

73
00:04:19,304 --> 00:04:19,904
does not.

74
00:04:19,304 --> 00:04:22,444
She reads the Besumer case as a domestic assault,

75
00:04:22,495 --> 00:04:25,774
and the Schneider case as a burglary that went wrong.

76
00:04:25,824 --> 00:04:28,164
So when you hear a number for this case,

77
00:04:28,214 --> 00:04:30,674
ask which list it comes from.

78
00:04:30,724 --> 00:04:33,164
The papers also reached backward.

79
00:04:33,214 --> 00:04:36,504
An Italian detective told the Times-Picayune

80
00:04:36,554 --> 00:04:39,964
the killings looked like a series from nineteen eleven.

81
00:04:40,014 --> 00:04:43,424
Recent researchers are not sure those belong either.

82
00:04:43,474 --> 00:04:45,824
The list was growing in both directions.

83
00:04:46,466 --> 00:04:51,846
March, nineteen nineteen. Gretna, across the river.

84
00:04:51,896 --> 00:04:55,746
Charles and Rosie Cortimiglia were grocers.

85
00:04:55,796 --> 00:04:58,786
Their daughter, Mary, was two years old.

86
00:04:58,836 --> 00:05:03,136
Around three in the morning, someone came into their home.

87
00:05:03,186 --> 00:05:05,676
The records do not agree on the night.

88
00:05:05,726 --> 00:05:09,506
One says March ninth. One says March tenth.

89
00:05:09,556 --> 00:05:12,946
Charles and Rosie were struck with an axe.

90
00:05:12,996 --> 00:05:14,616
Both survived.

91
00:05:14,666 --> 00:05:16,876
Mary was killed.

92
00:05:16,926 --> 00:05:20,406
A panel of the back door had been chiseled out.

93
00:05:20,456 --> 00:05:22,936
The axe was on the back porch.

94
00:05:22,986 --> 00:05:23,966
Nothing was taken.

95
00:05:29,704 --> 00:05:34,394
A few days later, a letter reached the Times-Picayune.

96
00:05:34,444 --> 00:05:36,674
It came in the Friday mail.

97
00:05:36,724 --> 00:05:41,884
It was dated, "Hell, March thirteenth, nineteen nineteen."

98
00:05:41,934 --> 00:05:45,464
It was signed, "The Axman."

99
00:05:45,514 --> 00:05:49,284
The paper printed it that Sunday, March sixteenth.

100
00:05:49,334 --> 00:05:53,954
Many retellings say it ran on the thirteenth, the date at the top.

101
00:05:54,004 --> 00:05:57,464
The newspaper's own page says the sixteenth.

102
00:05:57,514 --> 00:06:02,104
"They have never caught me," it began, "and they never will."

103
00:06:02,154 --> 00:06:07,174
The writer called himself a fell demon from hottest hell.

104
00:06:07,224 --> 00:06:11,694
He said he would come at twelve fifteen on the following Tuesday night.

105
00:06:11,744 --> 00:06:16,954
He said he would pass over any house with a jazz band in full swing.

106
00:06:17,004 --> 00:06:20,964
And he said the rest might get the axe.

107
00:06:21,014 --> 00:06:23,988
The paper said the handwriting looked like other letters

108
00:06:24,038 --> 00:06:27,334
sent to the superintendent of police during the investigation.

109
00:06:27,384 --> 00:06:31,884
It also said the letter would get the investigation it seemed to warrant.

110
00:06:31,934 --> 00:06:33,734
That is all the paper knew.

111
00:06:33,784 --> 00:06:36,714
It is all anyone has known since.

112
00:06:36,764 --> 00:06:40,894
The letter has never been tied to the person who attacked the Cortimiglias.

113
00:06:41,354 --> 00:06:43,344
So the city played.

114
00:06:43,394 --> 00:06:47,124
The next morning, the Times-Picayune ran a cartoon:

115
00:06:47,174 --> 00:06:50,834
a family at home, playing as hard as they could.

116
00:06:50,884 --> 00:06:53,534
Then the letter became a song.

117
00:06:53,584 --> 00:06:56,474
A local composer, Joseph John Davilla, published

118
00:06:56,524 --> 00:06:58,224
"The Mysterious Axman's Jazz."

119
00:06:58,274 --> 00:07:00,314
The cover borrowed the newspaper's cartoon.

120
00:07:00,364 --> 00:07:03,604
By May, it was being advertised in the same paper.

121
00:07:03,654 --> 00:07:08,054
Here is the question nobody can settle.

122
00:07:08,104 --> 00:07:16,384
Who benefits from a letter that tells a whole city to hire a band?

123
00:07:16,434 --> 00:07:20,284
Miriam Davis argues the Axeman did not write it.

124
00:07:20,334 --> 00:07:24,804
The language, she says, is too polished for the man she found in the record.

125
00:07:24,854 --> 00:07:28,534
She has named the composer as her likeliest candidate.

126
00:07:28,584 --> 00:07:30,404
That is a theory.

127
00:07:30,454 --> 00:07:32,064
It is not a finding.

128
00:07:32,114 --> 00:07:34,804
No one has ever matched the handwriting to anyone.

129
00:07:35,528 --> 00:07:40,978
While the city was dancing, Rosie Cortimiglia was in the hospital.

130
00:07:41,028 --> 00:07:46,378
When she could speak, she named the men she said attacked them.

131
00:07:46,428 --> 00:07:51,328
Her neighbors. Iorlando Jordano, a grocer in his late sixties,

132
00:07:51,378 --> 00:07:54,398
and his son, Frank, who was seventeen.

133
00:07:54,448 --> 00:07:58,248
Iorlando had answered the family's screams that night.

134
00:07:58,298 --> 00:08:01,788
Charles Cortimiglia said they were not the men.

135
00:08:01,838 --> 00:08:04,948
Rosie could not read or write English.

136
00:08:04,998 --> 00:08:08,948
On March twenty-ninth, she signed a statement naming them.

137
00:08:08,998 --> 00:08:11,278
The trial began in May.

138
00:08:11,328 --> 00:08:14,138
On May twenty-sixth, nineteen nineteen, the jury found both men guilty.

139
00:08:14,188 --> 00:08:17,008
Frank was sentenced to hang.

140
00:08:17,058 --> 00:08:21,958
His father was sentenced to life.

141
00:08:22,008 --> 00:08:25,248
Eight months later, Rosie went to a reporter.

142
00:08:25,298 --> 00:08:27,988
She said her accusation was false.

143
00:08:28,038 --> 00:08:31,028
She said she had not recognized the attackers.

144
00:08:31,078 --> 00:08:34,808
She told him Saint Joseph had come to her in a dream.

145
00:08:34,858 --> 00:08:39,028
She later said under oath that a jailer had threatened her.

146
00:08:39,078 --> 00:08:44,858
In April, nineteen twenty, the Louisiana Supreme Court overturned both convictions.

147
00:08:44,908 --> 00:08:47,508
In December, the charges were dropped.

148
00:08:47,558 --> 00:08:49,118
The Jordanos went home.

149
00:08:54,686 --> 00:08:59,686
The jazz night had passed. The attacks had not stopped.

150
00:08:59,736 --> 00:09:06,766
August tenth, nineteen nineteen. Steve Boca, a grocer, attacked at home.

151
00:09:06,816 --> 00:09:12,206
A door panel chiseled out. He survived. He remembered nothing.

152
00:09:12,256 --> 00:09:17,896
September third. Sarah Laumann, nineteen, alone in her home.

153
00:09:17,946 --> 00:09:19,476
She survived. An axe was found outside.

154
00:09:19,526 --> 00:09:25,806
October twenty-seventh. Mike Pepitone, killed in his bed,

155
00:09:25,856 --> 00:09:29,846
while his wife and children were in the house.

156
00:09:29,896 --> 00:09:34,146
In most retellings, he is the last victim of the Axeman.

157
00:09:34,196 --> 00:09:37,716
Miriam Davis says he was not killed with an axe at all,

158
00:09:37,766 --> 00:09:41,336
and she places his death in a different kind of violence.

159
00:09:41,386 --> 00:09:43,456
So the last victim on the list

160
00:09:43,506 --> 00:09:44,476
may not belong on it.

161
00:09:45,113 --> 00:09:49,273
There is an ending most people have heard.

162
00:09:49,323 --> 00:09:53,833
In it, Pepitone's widow moves to Los Angeles.

163
00:09:53,883 --> 00:09:58,093
In December, nineteen twenty, she shoots a man there.

164
00:09:58,143 --> 00:10:00,713
His name is given as Joseph Mumfre, or Monfre, or Manfre.

165
00:10:00,763 --> 00:10:03,773
She says he was the Axeman, and that he killed her husband.

166
00:10:03,823 --> 00:10:08,993
It is a neat ending.

167
00:10:09,043 --> 00:10:17,113
I have not been able to confirm it in a court record, or in a newspaper from that year.

168
00:10:17,163 --> 00:10:20,063
Some writers cite a Los Angeles paper.

169
00:10:20,113 --> 00:10:22,533
I have not seen that page.

170
00:10:22,583 --> 00:10:28,723
Miriam Davis says the man named in the story was in prison during most of the attacks.

171
00:10:28,773 --> 00:10:33,863
Until the page is found, the ending is a story about the case,

172
00:10:33,913 --> 00:10:34,643
not part of it.

173
00:10:40,116 --> 00:10:43,326
So here is what the record holds.

174
00:10:43,376 --> 00:10:49,136
Some families were attacked at night, in their homes, most of them grocers.

175
00:10:49,186 --> 00:10:51,586
Back door panels were cut out.

176
00:10:51,636 --> 00:10:55,036
Axes, often their own, were left behind.

177
00:10:55,086 --> 00:10:57,796
Usually, nothing was taken.

178
00:10:57,846 --> 00:10:59,806
Here is what was added.

179
00:10:59,856 --> 00:11:02,146
A number that keeps changing.

180
00:11:02,196 --> 00:11:09,076
The letter, as the paper described it, spoke of five murders. One recent count says eight dead.

181
00:11:09,126 --> 00:11:13,326
Encyclopedias give anywhere from six to seventeen.

182
00:11:13,376 --> 00:11:18,036
A chalk message, an earlier series, a final victim,

183
00:11:18,086 --> 00:11:21,186
and a widow with a gun in California.

184
00:11:21,236 --> 00:11:25,196
And a letter from Hell, which has never been tied to the killer,

185
00:11:25,246 --> 00:11:27,856
and which sold a lot of music.

186
00:11:27,906 --> 00:11:32,296
The only two people the courts convicted, a seventeen-year-old and his father,

187
00:11:32,346 --> 00:11:35,666
were convicted on a statement the witness later took back.

188
00:11:35,716 --> 00:11:38,866
No conviction for these attacks has ever stood.

189
00:11:38,916 --> 00:11:42,386
Everyone named here who was suspected or charged

190
00:11:42,436 --> 00:11:43,926
is presumed innocent.

191
00:11:43,976 --> 00:11:47,806
This film is drawn from newspapers of the time and later court records.

192
00:11:47,856 --> 00:11:49,986
A report is not proof.

193
00:11:50,036 --> 00:11:53,826
And the next time someone tells you the Axeman loved jazz,

194
00:11:53,876 --> 00:11:55,466
ask them who wrote the letter.
